  "order": 5,
  "title": "A major triad on any root",
  "summary": "Count 4 half steps, then 3 more, and you can build a major triad anywhere.",
  "minutes": 6,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Count 4, then 3",
      "body": "From any root, count up 4 half steps for the 3rd, then 3 more for the 5th. The white keys do not always give you the right notes: E major needs G♯, and A♭ major needs C and E♭.\n\n**Why it matters:** bands play in every key, so you need every major triad, not just the white-key ones.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        64,
        68,
        71
      ],
      "labels": {
        "64": "E",
        "68": "G♯",
        "71": "B"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "A♭ major",
      "body": "Root A♭, 3rd C, 5th E♭.",
